00:40:37,015 --> 00:40:43,995
This is where we would have our
financial AI agent database schema

404
00:40:46,735 --> 00:40:49,365
We start by creating extensions.

405
00:40:49,815 --> 00:40:52,915
These are PostgreSQL extensions.

406
00:40:53,115 --> 00:40:57,835
These are PostgreSQL add-ons that
provide additional functionality

407
00:41:19,013 --> 00:41:25,053
The if not exists clause ensures that we
don't get errors in case the extension is,

408
00:41:25,113 --> 00:41:28,833
uh, in case the extension already exists.

409
00:41:29,383 --> 00:41:32,553
Vector is the pgvector extension.

410
00:41:33,133 --> 00:41:37,953
It provides vector data types and
operator for similarity search.

411
00:41:38,003 --> 00:41:43,143
This is the foundation of RAG,
Retrieval-Augmented Generation

412
00:41:46,667 --> 00:41:52,137
The uuid-ossp provides
UUID generation functions.

413
00:41:52,627 --> 00:41:57,977
We use the UUIDs as primary
key in all our tables.

414
00:41:58,267 --> 00:42:03,167
The UUIDs are globally unique
and do not require coordination

415
00:42:03,177 --> 00:42:04,987
between the different systems.

416
00:42:08,547 --> 00:42:13,817
The pg_trgm provides trigram
similarity for text search.

417
00:42:14,137 --> 00:42:19,777
It enables fuzzy text matching,
which is useful for fallback search

418
00:42:19,827 --> 00:42:22,237
when vector search isn't enough

419
00:42:30,037 --> 00:42:36,717
The Btree_gene provides gene
indexes for composite data types.

420
00:42:36,817 --> 00:42:41,547
This allows efficient
indexing of JSONB columns.

421
00:42:42,057 --> 00:42:45,927
JSONB is used for metrics and entities.

422
00:43:00,305 --> 00:43:03,185
Now, let's take a look
at the filings table.

423
00:43:03,795 --> 00:43:08,695
The filings table will store
metadata about each SEC filing.

424
00:43:08,695 --> 00:43:09,105
This is

425
00:43:11,755 --> 00:43:15,835
the single source of truth
for what we've ingested.

426
00:43:16,605 --> 00:43:21,015
Each filing has a unique
file hash for deduplication.

427
00:43:21,655 --> 00:43:25,445
The ID column is a UUID primary key.

428
00:43:26,065 --> 00:43:29,165
We use generate_random_uuid to
generate UUIDs automatically.

429
00:43:29,165 --> 00:43:29,705
This is

430
00:43:34,345 --> 00:43:38,825
to avoid conflict when inserting
from different systems.

431
00:43:39,565 --> 00:43:43,365
Ticker is the stock
ticker symbol like AAPL

432
00:43:45,685 --> 00:43:47,665
or MSFT.

433
00:43:49,585 --> 00:43:51,876
It's varchar of ten.

434
00:43:53,675 --> 00:44:00,475
It's of variable character of ten,
which is sufficient for all tickers.

435
00:44:01,115 --> 00:44:05,635
It's not null because every
filing belong to a company.

436
00:44:11,056 --> 00:44:19,676
Filing type is the SEC form type
like 10-K, 10-Q, 8-K that we use.

437
00:44:20,466 --> 00:44:25,316
We use a check constraint to ensure
only valid types are inserted.

438
00:44:26,126 --> 00:44:28,496
This prevents data corruption.

439
00:44:29,676 --> 00:44:36,116
File hash is the SHA-256 hash
of the raw filing contents.

440
00:44:36,506 --> 00:44:42,846
It's unique, which ensures that
we never store duplicated filings.

441
00:44:43,506 --> 00:44:46,156
This is our deduplication mechanism.

442
00:44:46,875 --> 00:44:50,916
Is active is a boolean for soft deletes.

443
00:44:52,096 --> 00:44:56,725
Instead of deleting rows,
we set is active to false.

444
00:44:56,886 --> 00:45:01,306
This preserve data for
auditing and recovery purposes.

445
00:46:18,222 --> 00:46:23,222
We create indexes on the
filings table for performance

446
00:46:26,084 --> 00:46:28,104
The index_filings_ticker.

447
00:46:30,484 --> 00:46:36,094
The index_filing_ticker helps
with queries like find all filings

448
00:46:36,144 --> 00:46:40,384
for AAPL in order of fiscal year.

449
00:46:42,344 --> 00:46:47,404
The index_filing_type_year helps
with queries like find all 10-K

450
00:46:47,404 --> 00:46:50,454
filings sorted by fiscal year.

451
00:46:51,234 --> 00:46:56,404
This is useful for retrieving
the latest filing of a type.

452
00:46:57,604 --> 00:47:02,613
The index_filings_active
is a partial index.

453
00:47:03,063 --> 00:47:07,104
It only indexes rows
where is_active is true.

454
00:47:08,583 --> 00:47:13,313
This keeps the index small
and fast for active records,

455
00:47:13,694 --> 00:47:18,784
The financial chunks table is
the heart of our AI system.

456
00:47:19,444 --> 00:47:25,884
Each row stores a chunk of text from a
filing along with its vector embedding.

457
00:47:26,444 --> 00:47:30,363
The filing ID is a foreign
key to the filings table.

458
00:47:31,294 --> 00:47:36,864
On delete cascade means that when
a filing is deleted, all its chunks

459
00:47:36,934 --> 00:47:39,534
are automatically deleted as well.

460
00:47:41,374 --> 00:47:46,114
The embedding column uses
vector one five three six.

461
00:47:46,683 --> 00:47:51,303
This is the PG vector
type with 1536 dimensions.

462
00:47:54,013 --> 00:47:58,453
This matches the OpenAI text embedding
three small model dimensions.

463
00:48:08,087 --> 00:48:10,797
The metrics column is JSONB.

464
00:48:12,107 --> 00:48:20,117
It stores extracted financial metrics
like revenue, net income, EPS, and margin.

465
00:48:20,777 --> 00:48:26,157
JSONB allows flexible schema
and efficient querying.

466
00:48:26,967 --> 00:48:29,687
The entities column is JSONB.

467
00:48:30,267 --> 00:48:38,017
It stores extracted entities like company
names, people, dates, and amounts.

468
00:48:38,557 --> 00:48:41,787
This will be populated
by the NER pipeline.

469
00:48:42,837 --> 00:48:50,067
The sentiment score is a floating
point between -1.0 and 1.0.

470
00:48:50,067 --> 00:48:54,677
Negative values indicates a
negative sentiment, while the

471
00:48:54,727 --> 00:49:00,337
positive value indicates a positive
sentiment, a zero being neutral.

472
00:49:06,401 --> 00:49:14,161
We create multiple indexes on the
financial chunks table for fast retrieval.

473
00:49:14,841 --> 00:49:23,861
The idx, the index chunk ticker,
the index chunk ticker year helps

474
00:49:23,891 --> 00:49:25,851
with ticker-based filtering.

475
00:49:26,711 --> 00:49:33,531
The index chunk filing section helps
with filtering by filing and section.

476
00:49:33,941 --> 00:49:40,071
This allows us to retrieve all chunks
from a specific section of a filing.

477
00:49:40,081 --> 00:49:47,671
The index chunk metrics uses
GIN on the jsonb metrics column.

478
00:49:48,161 --> 00:49:53,241
This enables efficient filtering
on metrics value like revenue

479
00:49:53,641 --> 00:49:55,711
greater than one hundred million.

480
00:49:56,451 --> 00:49:56,761
The

481
00:49:59,201 --> 00:50:00,581
index chunk text

482
00:50:03,061 --> 00:50:06,081
ti-trigram uses GIN with
the trigram operator.

483
00:50:06,541 --> 00:50:10,991
This enables a full text search
on the chunk text column.

484
00:50:12,071 --> 00:50:14,881
It is used by hybrid search fallback

485
00:50:23,997 --> 00:50:31,387
The analysis history table stores an
audit trail of every query and response.

486
00:50:31,807 --> 00:50:37,167
This is append only, that is, we
never update or delete these records.

487
00:50:37,677 --> 00:50:42,247
This preserves the complete history
for auditing and debugging purposes.

488
00:50:42,787 --> 00:50:43,397
The question and

489
00:50:46,397 --> 00:50:48,967
