386
00:30:40,908 --> 00:30:42,588
Now let's write the first rule

387
00:30:43,588 --> 00:30:45,718
It denies the latest image tag.

388
00:30:46,278 --> 00:30:49,218
That is, this rule finds every container.

389
00:30:49,998 --> 00:30:54,038
If the image ends with latest, it denies.

390
00:30:54,858 --> 00:30:57,968
The message tells the
developer what to fix.

391
00:30:58,828 --> 00:31:00,678
Why is latest dangerous?

392
00:31:00,878 --> 00:31:02,078
Because it changes.

393
00:31:02,758 --> 00:31:05,948
Today, it might be version 1.0.

394
00:31:05,948 --> 00:31:09,168
Tomorrow, it might be version 1.1.

395
00:31:09,168 --> 00:31:11,068
You can't reproduce bugs.

396
00:31:11,678 --> 00:31:13,327
You can't roll back safely.

397
00:31:14,258 --> 00:31:16,347
Now, let's write the second rule

398
00:31:20,536 --> 00:31:22,806
It requires resource requests and limits

399
00:31:27,096 --> 00:31:32,816
Without CPU requests, the horizontal
pod autoscaler cannot function.

400
00:31:33,446 --> 00:31:36,546
It doesn't know how
much CPU each pod uses.

401
00:31:36,926 --> 00:31:38,156
It can't scale properly

402
00:31:42,894 --> 00:31:47,614
Next, let's deny if the message contains

403
00:31:56,620 --> 00:31:58,830
Next is the memory requests.

404
00:31:59,760 --> 00:32:02,520
The memory requests are equally important.

405
00:32:02,770 --> 00:32:07,050
Without them, the scheduler
can't make placement decisions.

406
00:32:07,540 --> 00:32:10,930
Pods might run on nodes
without enough memory.

407
00:32:11,470 --> 00:32:12,920
Now let's write the third rule.

408
00:32:13,670 --> 00:32:16,730
It requires the read-only
root file system.

409
00:32:22,852 --> 00:32:25,322
This is a security best practice.

410
00:32:26,042 --> 00:32:30,272
All containers must have the
read-only root file system.

411
00:32:31,642 --> 00:32:34,592
This prevents post-exploit persistence.

412
00:32:34,912 --> 00:32:37,772
If an attacker gets in, they can't write.

413
00:32:38,402 --> 00:32:40,702
Now, let's write the fourth rule.

414
00:32:41,562 --> 00:32:45,692
It requires allow privilege
escalation to be false.

415
00:32:46,172 --> 00:32:50,812
This prevents setuid binaries
from escalating privileges.

416
00:32:51,641 --> 00:32:56,272
If-- it blocks a common
container escape technique.

417
00:32:56,682 --> 00:32:58,952
No privilege escalation is allowed.

418
00:32:59,572 --> 00:33:01,462
Now, let's write the fifth rule.

419
00:33:01,972 --> 00:33:04,482
It requires capabilities to be dropped.

420
00:33:05,022 --> 00:33:07,032
All capabilities must be dropped.

421
00:33:07,542 --> 00:33:10,762
No container needs
capabilities in production.

422
00:33:11,192 --> 00:33:13,982
This reduces the attack
surface significantly.

423
00:33:14,622 --> 00:33:16,702
Now, let's write the sixth rule.

424
00:33:17,252 --> 00:33:20,182
It requires running as a non-root user.

425
00:33:21,182 --> 00:33:22,962
Running as root is dangerous.

426
00:33:23,192 --> 00:33:27,342
If an attacker com- compromises
the container, they have root

427
00:33:27,472 --> 00:33:29,102
privileges on the container.

